From f9db60a6da900a6cfb65e6a9bbd588bf4a80e632 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: caoanhhao <caoanhhao@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 13:17:23 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] firmware: qcom: qseecom: /dev/qseecom for user-space QSEE
 apps

Device HALs reach their trusted apps (e.g. Samsung's fingerprint app on
the Galaxy Tab S7) through Qualcomm's downstream /dev/qseecom, an ABI
mainline does not have. Add a small one of its own, on an auxiliary
"user" device the qseecom driver now always creates:

- QSEECOM_IOC_APP_LOAD binds the file to an app by name. If the secure
  world does not run it yet, the app is started from the split image the
  caller passes (.mdt + .bXX as read from the modem partition), copied
  into a TZ memory pool of its own for the call.
- QSEECOM_IOC_APP_SEND calls the app with request/response buffers of at
  most 1 MiB each. Both are copied in (callers preset response fields)
  to one TZ buffer, the response right after the request, as the apps'
  shared buffer is laid out downstream; the secure world refuses a
  request and a response from two separate allocations.
- QSEECOM_IOC_APP_UNLOAD or close drops the client. Apps are never shut
  down: on the Galaxy Tab S7 LTE starting an app again after shutting it
  down reset the SoC, so an app keeps running until reboot and a
  restarted client binds to it again.

